diff --git a/projects/patchbay/hardware-annotations-verified-only.md b/projects/patchbay/hardware-annotations-verified-only.md
--- a/projects/patchbay/hardware-annotations-verified-only.md
+++ b/projects/patchbay/hardware-annotations-verified-only.md
@@ -12,44 +12,47 @@
 
 ## statement
 
-A Scarlett annotation (`hardware source: ...`) appears on a PipeWire port only when all of the following hold: the port carries an ALSA PCM path in `object.path` (`alsa:pcm:…:capture:capture_N`), from which its channel index is read; the node's device hardware serial matches an open card whose topology profile SessionMixer detected for that firmware; the card's event monitor is running; and the most recent `RefreshFromHardware` for that channel's endpoint succeeded. Otherwise the inspector shows `unavailable` with the reason. The join between port and PCM channel is the kernel driver's own indexing, `capture_AUXn` to `pcm-capture-(n+1)`: PipeWire's path gives the channel index on the ALSA capture PCM and the scarlett2 driver names its routing controls by one-based channel on the same PCM. It is never derived from the `AUXn` text in a port name, from a neighbor, or from any resemblance between labels, and a port without a PCM path is not annotated. SessionMixer's cached `GetCurrentSource` is never read for a channel the adapter holds invalid, because that cache keeps its last value through a failed read. The adapter watches through `EventMonitor.Watch` and refreshes every verified endpoint itself on each event; `WatchControls` is not used, because it skips unreadable controls silently. Annotations are read-only: nothing in Patchbay writes a Scarlett control. The whole package is conditional: if it needs a SessionMixer or scarlettctl change, or outgrows a small addition, it stops and the scope returns to the operator.
+A Scarlett hardware-source annotation uses the port's ALSA PCM capture path in `object.path` to identify a channel on PCM device 0. Its owning PipeWire device reports a hardware serial and `api.alsa.card`; the adapter opens that card, verifies it is a Scarlett, and detects its model and firmware through SessionMixer's topology. The card number in the port's path must match that device's card number. The card API exposes no hardware serial, so this is a join through PipeWire's card number, not a comparison with a serial read from ALSA.
+
+The channel index N maps to `pcm-capture-(N+1)` by the kernel driver's indexing. It is looked up by endpoint port number within the detected profile, never by slice position, the `AUXn` text in a display name, or resemblance between labels. A non-Scarlett device is not annotated; an eligible port whose join or read cannot be established shows unavailable with a reason.
+
+The adapter owns validity. Its last completed refresh must have succeeded for every tracked endpoint, and monitoring must not have stopped or failed or the card closed. The two accepted display windows are explicit: the first successful read may be shown before Watch begins polling because the handle subscribed at open, and the last successful state may remain visible during an event's re-read. A completed failed refresh invalidates all channels until a successful refresh; a stopped monitor or closed card is not repaired merely by cached values. The card's lifetime belongs to one PipeWire device object in one connection session.
+
+The current adapter uses `EventMonitor.Watch` and refreshes every verified endpoint on an event because Watch reports no useful control id. `WatchControls` silently skips unreadable controls and does not satisfy this validity policy. Annotations are read-only: nothing in Patchbay writes a Scarlett control.
 
 ## why
 
-The spec forbids a guessed route displayed as fact and stale hardware state displayed as current, in those words. The plausible correspondence — `capture_AUX3` is PCM capture 4 — is exactly the kind of ordering argument that is usually right and occasionally, after a profile change or a firmware update, wrong in a way no one notices because the annotation keeps looking sensible. scarlettctl's and SessionMixer's conveniences were written for a control surface that owns the card and can afford optimism; Patchbay borrows them for a claim about truth and has to add the pessimism itself.
+The useful promise is an established port-to-channel join and a successful hardware read, with ordinary observation latency. SessionMixer retains cached values after failed reads and can omit endpoints from its slice, so Patchbay must check validity and look up channels by port number. Neither those checks nor the driver-index join require blanking the display while a read is in progress or routing a signal through every channel to establish the mapping again.
 
 ## discrimination
 
-Flag an annotation produced for a port with no ALSA PCM path, for a device whose topology profile was not detected, or for a channel index outside the detected profile's PCM capture count. Flag any code that derives the channel from the `AUXn` text of a port name or alias, from adjacency to another port, or from a port's position in a list rather than from the `object.path` channel index. Flag a `SourceFor` that returns true after a failed refresh, after the monitor stopped or errored, or after the card closed, without an intervening successful read. Flag use of `WatchControls`, or an event handler that refreshes only the endpoint matching the reported numid (the current `Watch` always reports 0). Flag any call to a scarlettctl setter or SessionMixer routing mutator from Patchbay. Flag documentation that presents the join as verified by signal when it is derived from driver indexing, or that omits the basis.
+Flag an annotation based on an unestablished PCM channel/card join, an undetected topology profile, a channel outside the profile, or a positional endpoint lookup. Flag deriving the channel from a port name, alias, neighboring port, or list position instead of its PCM path. Flag a successful SourceFor result after a completed failed refresh, after the monitor stopped or errored, or after the card closed; recovery requires both a valid monitoring lifecycle and successful reads. Flag annotations carried into a replacement device or connection using the previous card's validity. Flag cached source lookup for a channel held invalid, use of WatchControls under its current silent-skip behavior, or refreshing only a reported numid when Watch reports 0. Flag Scarlett setters or SessionMixer routing mutators called by Patchbay. Flag documentation claiming signal verification when the join comes from driver indexing.
 
-Do not flag `unavailable` being shown often; that is the honest default. Do not flag the inspector showing SessionMixer's cached value *for a valid channel* immediately after a successful refresh. Do not flag an optional operator spot-check that routes a signal to confirm the join; it is outside the application and not a prerequisite.
+Do not flag the first successful read shown before Watch starts polling on the already-subscribed handle, or the last successful state shown during an in-progress re-read. These are accepted observation windows, not missing invalidation steps. Do not require an ALSA-side hardware serial the dependency does not expose, an additional start pre-check, an arbitrary waiting period, or routine signal testing of the driver-defined channel join. Do not flag unavailable for an established failure.
 
 ## example
 
-Met:
+Met (after verifying the device, card, PCM path, and profile):
 
 ```go
-func (a *Adapter) SourceFor(p pipewire.Port) (string, bool) {
-    n, ok := pcmChannelIndex(p.ObjectPath)   // alsa:pcm:1:hw:1:capture:capture_3 -> 3
-    if !ok || !a.monitoring {
-        return "", false
-    }
-    ep := a.endpointByPort[n+1]              // pcm-capture-(n+1) looked up by port number: SessionMixer's slice skips unreadable channels, so position is not channel
-    if ep == nil || !a.valid[n] {
-        return "", false
-    }
-    return ep.GetCurrentSource(), true
+// n comes from the PCM capture path; endpoint slices may omit channels
+if !c.monitoring || !c.valid {
+    return "unavailable", false
 }
+ep := c.endpoints[n+1] // keyed by pcm-capture-(n+1), not slice position
+if ep == nil {
+    return "no routing control for this channel", false
+}
+return ep.source()
 ```
 
 Fallen short:
 
 ```go
-// parse the channel out of the display name
-n := auxIndex(port.Name)                     // "capture_AUX3" -> 3, from the label, not the PCM path
-return device.PCMCaptureEndpoints[n].GetCurrentSource(), true // positional index into a slice that skips channels; no validity, no monitor check
+n := auxIndex(port.Name) // derives the channel from a display label
+return device.PCMCaptureEndpoints[n].GetCurrentSource(), true // skipped channels and failed reads are ignored
 ```
 
 ## boundary
 
-This binds what is displayed as a hardware route and under what evidence. The join is established from driver indexing rather than measured; the quality's teeth are the PCM-path source of the index, the detected profile, and the validity rule, not a table. It does not bind SessionMixer or scarlettctl themselves, which keep their own conventions. The size condition ("a small addition") is the operator's judgment, made when the stage is taken up; the quality's job is to catch the inference and the staleness, not to measure the package.
+This binds the evidence for a displayed hardware source, read-only access, and the accepted validity lifecycle. It does not demand instantaneous agreement with hardware between observations. The first-read and in-refresh windows above are settled decisions, not gaps to close with more state. The adapter's size was accepted at stage 7; the original conditional size stop is retired. This quality does not govern SessionMixer or scarlettctl internals or require Patchbay to change those dependencies.
