 ## statement
 
-Every `import "C"`, every libpipewire call, every proxy, hook, and `pw_properties` lives in `internal/pipewire`, and every libpipewire call there executes on the connection's `pw_thread_loop` thread under its lock. Macro wrappers and listener tables live in the package's `pipewire.c`/`pipewire.h`; the Go side exports only callback trampolines. No Go pointer is handed to C storage; one `cgo.Handle` per connection carries the Go side into callbacks. The rest of the application reads the backend through `Snapshot()` (an immutable value replaced by pointer swap, never mutated in place), drains `Events()`, and posts requests; it never holds a native handle, and nothing outside the package names a `C.` type or a PipeWire property key as a transport detail.
+Native PipeWire access belongs to `internal/pipewire`: its cgo transport, libpipewire calls, proxies, hooks, and properties never escape to the UI or model. Macro wrappers and listener tables live in `pipewire.c`/`pipewire.h`; callback trampolines translate observations into Go inputs. No retained Go pointer is handed to C storage; one `cgo.Handle` per connection carries the Go side into callbacks.
+
+The backend owns the connection lifecycle and obeys each native operation's threading requirements. Graph callbacks and posted requests run on the connection's thread loop. Other backend lifecycle operations may take the loop lock; initialization before the loop starts and teardown after it stops need no nonexistent concurrent loop owner. Operations documented as safe from another thread, including the loop wake used to post work, may run there. Native handles remain alive for every such operation, and the loop-lock-before-backend-lock order is preserved.
+
+The rest of the application reads an immutable `Snapshot()` and posts requests. A published snapshot and its maps are never mutated by either publisher or reader. Optional change notifications are hints, not an additional source of truth or a required delivery channel. Model and UI decisions use typed snapshot fields; the inspector may display raw properties read-only.
 
 ## why
 
-libpipewire is not thread-safe outside its loop, dfx draws on its own thread every frame, and cgo adds its own rules about pointer lifetime. The one place those three meet is the backend boundary, and the spec draws it deliberately: native transport details must not become application state or workspace identity. An immutable snapshot is what lets the UI read without a lock and lets the model be tested from a sample file with no PipeWire present at all. A `C.` type or a raw property lookup leaking upward is the first step toward the model depending on the daemon being live.
