 ## statement
 
-Every live PipeWire object the backend tracks — node, port, link, device, client, metrics record, pending request target, in-session provenance entry — is keyed and cross-referenced by its `object.serial`. The numeric protocol id is a transient handle used only to address the server in a request (`pw_registry_bind`, `pw_registry_destroy`, link-factory properties), and it is read at the moment of the call, never stored as identity or used as a map key. Where PipeWire itself hands back only an id (profiler blocks, link-factory properties, a proxy's bound global id), the backend resolves it to the serial of the object currently holding that id and guards the resolution against the id having been reused since.
+Retained live object identity uses `object.serial` within one connection session: nodes, ports, links, devices, clients, pending request targets, provenance, and metrics records must not transfer to a replacement merely because PipeWire reused its numeric id. A reconnect starts a new session even when its serials repeat.
+
+Protocol ids are necessary transport handles. The backend may store them on objects, receive them in callbacks, and maintain an id-to-current-serial index. Node-to-device, port-to-node, and link-endpoint references resolve to serials at the referring object's announcement, rather than repeatedly joining to whichever object later holds the id. Link requests name endpoint or target serials and the caller's session; the backend reads the observed object's id when addressing PipeWire. Create confirmation checks the captured link's endpoint serials against the request.
+
+Profiler observations name nodes only by id. Their records are serial-keyed and cleared on removal and disconnect, but attribution of buffered pods has the explicit limit below. That diagnostic limit is not permission to use an id as a routing target's retained identity.
 
 ## why
 
-The captured samples show ids recycling within seconds: a REAPER restart brought the new instance back as the same node id and the same client id, with only the serial advanced, and its port ids were reshuffled among its own ports. A map keyed by id silently merges two lifetimes — positions, provenance, baselines, and pending requests from a departed object land on its replacement and are shown as fact. Serials are the only runtime-unique handle PipeWire offers, and even they can repeat across a daemon restart, which is why reconnect clears state explicitly rather than trusting difference.
+The captured samples show ids recycling within seconds: a REAPER restart brought the new instance back as the same node id and the same client id, with only the serial advanced, and its port ids were reshuffled among its own ports. Retained application state keyed by id silently merges two lifetimes — positions, provenance, baselines, and pending requests from a departed object land on its replacement and are shown as fact. Serials are the only runtime-unique handle PipeWire offers, and even they can repeat across a daemon restart, which is why reconnect clears state explicitly rather than trusting difference.
 
